  [1mB. Native ES modules, no bundler.[0m [38;5;153msession.js[39m uses [38;5;153mexport[39m; [38;5;153mindex.html[39m loads [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m; [38;5;153mapp.js
[39m  imports what it needs. [3mTrade-off:[0m a real enforced boundary — a form can't use the identity without importing it, and
  the import is the documentation. Native in every current browser, still zero dependencies and zero build step. Two
  real costs: modules are fetched under CORS rules, so [1mopening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m by double-clicking it from disk stops working[0m
  — you'd need any static server ([38;5;153mpython3 -m http.server[39m) — and module scope means [38;5;153mapp.js[39m's functions are no longer
  globals, so inline [38;5;153monclick=[39m handlers would break. Neither applies to the current code, but the [38;5;153mfile://[39m one might apply
  to [3myou[0m.

  [1mC. Add a bundler.[0m esbuild or Vite, one entry point, and the currently-orphaned CommonJS [38;5;153msrc/[39m half gets folded in with
  the browser half. [3mTrade-off:[0m this is the only option that addresses the repo's actual structural oddity — two
  disconnected module systems that never meet — and it's the one that brings a test runner and npm within reach. But it
  adds dependencies, a lockfile, and a build you must run before you can look at the page, to a repo whose entire
  browser surface is one 28-line file. That's a lot of machinery bought on the strength of one login form.

  [1mMy recommendation: B[0m, conditional on one thing. You told me the requirement is reuse across forms that don't exist
  yet; B is the only option under which that reuse is enforced rather than remembered, and it costs nothing in
  dependencies. C is the same direction with a much larger down payment that I don't think one form justifies yet — and
  B is a clean on-ramp to C if you later want it. A is genuinely cheaper [3mtoday[0m and I'd pick it if this were a one-off,
  but you explicitly said it isn't.

  The condition: if you currently open [38;5;153mindex.html[39m by double-clicking the file, B breaks that and A doesn't. That's the
  one thing I can't determine from the repo.
